WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.290 --> 00:00:05.290
Nigeria has a cash problem.

00:00:06.130 --> 00:00:08.220
I mean, it's the richest country in Africa

00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:10.763
with a nominal GDP of over $400 billion.

00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:15.800
But when it comes to making transactions,

00:00:15.800 --> 00:00:18.430
most locals pay with cash.

00:00:18.430 --> 00:00:21.160
In fact, only about 35% of Nigerians

00:00:21.160 --> 00:00:23.040
even have a bank account.

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:26.010
And with a population of
over 200 million people,

00:00:26.010 --> 00:00:28.750
that's a lot of paper floating around.

00:00:28.750 --> 00:00:32.090
And excess cash can lead to
increased crime, corruption,

00:00:32.090 --> 00:00:34.610
and a difficulty for some
Nigerians to accrue credit

00:00:34.610 --> 00:00:35.803
and obtain loans.

00:00:37.190 --> 00:00:40.000
So a tech entrepreneur named Tayo Oviosu

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:42.210
came up with an idea to
change the way Nigerians

00:00:42.210 --> 00:00:44.740
think about money, which
quickly set him on a path

00:00:44.740 --> 00:00:47.350
to becoming one of the
country's biggest rising stars

00:00:47.350 --> 00:00:48.203
of FinTech.

00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:56.903
I was born in Lagos,
Nigeria in West Africa.

00:00:59.010 --> 00:01:02.760
Growing up here, you know,
really was fantastic for me.

00:01:02.760 --> 00:01:05.350
I learned a lot in terms of just grit

00:01:05.350 --> 00:01:08.410
and learning to handle a variety of issues

00:01:08.410 --> 00:01:09.470
that you face every day

00:01:09.470 --> 00:01:13.193
where not all the
infrastructure work smoothly.

00:01:14.890 --> 00:01:17.230
I was raised by a single parent.

00:01:17.230 --> 00:01:22.230
My mom raised five kids, and
I was the last of five boys.

00:01:22.470 --> 00:01:26.670
I got to see firsthand at a very young age

00:01:26.670 --> 00:01:29.900
how she was putting in
so much to make ends meet

00:01:29.900 --> 00:01:33.373
for all of us and the
sacrifices that she was making.

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:36.790
Inspired by his mother's work ethic

00:01:36.790 --> 00:01:38.680
and emphasis on education,

00:01:38.680 --> 00:01:41.410
Oviosu moved to the United
States for his undergrad degree

00:01:41.410 --> 00:01:42.990
at the age of 16.

00:01:42.990 --> 00:01:45.400
He majored in electrical
engineering at USC

00:01:45.400 --> 00:01:46.750
and later received a master's

00:01:46.750 --> 00:01:49.050
at Stanford's Graduate School of Business.

00:01:49.050 --> 00:01:50.350
While I was at Stanford,

00:01:50.350 --> 00:01:53.850
one of the things that
really we got to question on

00:01:53.850 --> 00:01:57.480
is, you know, what really
matters to you, right? And why.

00:01:57.480 --> 00:01:59.240
What is it that you really wanna do?

00:01:59.240 --> 00:02:03.250
I narrowed it down to I wanted
to help bring ideas to life,

00:02:03.250 --> 00:02:04.980
first and foremost, on
the African continent,

00:02:04.980 --> 00:02:06.493
but just generally speaking.

00:02:07.510 --> 00:02:09.150
After working for a few tech companies

00:02:09.150 --> 00:02:11.660
in America, Oviosu headed back to Lagos

00:02:11.660 --> 00:02:14.210
on a search to find an idea
he could bring to life.

00:02:16.830 --> 00:02:19.430
And it didn't take long
before he found one.

00:02:19.430 --> 00:02:20.910
So if you look at emerging markets,

00:02:20.910 --> 00:02:24.950
about 95% of transactions
are done in cash.

00:02:24.950 --> 00:02:27.030
And this is very unsafe.

00:02:27.030 --> 00:02:30.030
Merchants who are selling
have to create a variety

00:02:30.030 --> 00:02:33.490
of processes within their
stores to prevent pilferage

00:02:33.490 --> 00:02:34.463
from their staff.

00:02:35.740 --> 00:02:37.287
When you're traveling with a lot of cash,

00:02:37.287 --> 00:02:38.890
you can face theft.

00:02:38.890 --> 00:02:39.880
And then for the economy,

00:02:39.880 --> 00:02:42.251
it cost somewhere
between five to 7% of GDP

00:02:42.251 --> 00:02:45.230
to clean, process all of that cash.

00:02:45.230 --> 00:02:48.340
In Nigeria, and in really
most emerging markets,

00:02:48.340 --> 00:02:50.855
the reason why cash is still king

00:02:50.855 --> 00:02:54.490
is, first and foremost,
poor banking infrastructure.

00:02:54.490 --> 00:02:57.830
So while Nigeria has 23 banks,

00:02:57.830 --> 00:03:01.560
they only combine to have
about 5,000 bank branches.

00:03:01.560 --> 00:03:04.163
So they don't have the
reach to the mass market.

00:03:05.150 --> 00:03:07.490
So it's not that people don't save,

00:03:07.490 --> 00:03:09.893
they just do so through
very informal means.

00:03:10.740 --> 00:03:13.510
If you go to someone who
is selling in the market,

00:03:13.510 --> 00:03:16.410
they are saving at what's
called a savings collector.

00:03:16.410 --> 00:03:17.960
So the savings collector

00:03:17.960 --> 00:03:20.880
is someone who basically
allows you to save money

00:03:20.880 --> 00:03:22.884
on a daily basis with them

00:03:22.884 --> 00:03:25.300
and sometimes you don't even
get any interest on your money.

00:03:25.300 --> 00:03:27.520
Well, people do this and
people save also underneath

00:03:27.520 --> 00:03:29.870
the mattress in the house
or different pop places

00:03:29.870 --> 00:03:31.740
they put money.

00:03:31.740 --> 00:03:33.747
And so, for me, looking
at that, I was like,

00:03:33.747 --> 00:03:35.500
"Wow! This is a huge problem."

00:03:35.500 --> 00:03:37.720
There's gotta be a better way to pay

00:03:37.720 --> 00:03:39.603
than me having to pay in cash.

00:03:41.300 --> 00:03:43.940
But Oviosu soon noticed something else.

00:03:43.940 --> 00:03:46.800
I have a phone, and I have a phone signal.

00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:49.240
And in fact I have multiple phones, right?

00:03:49.240 --> 00:03:51.280
Because most Nigerians
carry two to three phones.

00:03:51.280 --> 00:03:53.170
And if I don't have a phone
signal in one network,

00:03:53.170 --> 00:03:54.820
I have a phone sitting on another network.

00:03:54.820 --> 00:03:56.390
So why can't I pay with my phone?

00:03:56.390 --> 00:03:58.692
And that's really what
got me going, right?

00:03:58.692 --> 00:04:00.320
And then I soon quickly realized

00:04:00.320 --> 00:04:04.010
that the problem I had was
actually worse for most people.

00:04:04.010 --> 00:04:05.930
I was banking with two banks,

00:04:05.930 --> 00:04:07.870
but most people were
not even banked at all.

00:04:07.870 --> 00:04:12.120
So 61% of Nigerians are not banked.

00:04:12.120 --> 00:04:14.557
So I was like, "Wow,
this problem is huge."

00:04:16.550 --> 00:04:18.830
So Oviosu formed a startup called Paga,

00:04:18.830 --> 00:04:21.030
which would allow Nigerians
to make transactions

00:04:21.030 --> 00:04:23.720
with their phones among other services.

00:04:23.720 --> 00:04:25.420
But one of the challenges was to convince

00:04:25.420 --> 00:04:28.170
a cash-dependent customer
that paying with Paga

00:04:28.170 --> 00:04:29.620
was legitimate.

00:04:29.620 --> 00:04:31.730
It was very clear to
me that for us to reach

00:04:31.730 --> 00:04:35.670
the mass market we had to build a network

00:04:35.670 --> 00:04:37.590
of what we call agents.

00:04:37.590 --> 00:04:41.110
These are mom and pops,
pharmacies, grocery stores,

00:04:41.110 --> 00:04:44.640
people in the local
community that are trusted.

00:04:44.640 --> 00:04:46.560
The agents provided consumers locations

00:04:46.560 --> 00:04:49.030
where Paga was supported
while offering the agents

00:04:49.030 --> 00:04:51.050
a commission on transactions.

00:04:51.050 --> 00:04:53.300
So we had to, door to door,

00:04:53.300 --> 00:04:55.260
build out this distribution network,

00:04:55.260 --> 00:04:57.920
and we now have 26,000 agents.

00:04:57.920 --> 00:05:00.430
We have 16 million users on our platform.

00:05:00.430 --> 00:05:03.070
Last year, we did about $2 billion

00:05:03.070 --> 00:05:05.900
in transaction values
processed on our platform.

00:05:05.900 --> 00:05:08.040
And Paga services have evolved quite a bit

00:05:08.040 --> 00:05:09.753
since it started in 2009.

00:05:10.600 --> 00:05:14.350
With Paga, you can send
money to anyone in Nigeria

00:05:14.350 --> 00:05:16.430
using their mobile phone number,

00:05:16.430 --> 00:05:19.980
you can pay any utility bill,

00:05:19.980 --> 00:05:22.780
you can send money to any bank in Nigeria,

00:05:22.780 --> 00:05:25.810
instant, real-time credit
in the bank account,

00:05:25.810 --> 00:05:28.250
you can withdraw money from any bank.

00:05:28.250 --> 00:05:29.823
We've actually leapfrogged
some of the things

00:05:29.823 --> 00:05:32.820
that are not available in the West.

00:05:32.820 --> 00:05:34.430
Now, there have been
other successful mobile

00:05:34.430 --> 00:05:36.460
payment services on the African continent,

00:05:36.460 --> 00:05:38.940
like M-Pesa, a Kenyan
company with a footprint

00:05:38.940 --> 00:05:41.620
in parts of East Africa
and the Middle East.

00:05:41.620 --> 00:05:43.620
But Oviosu sees Paga as a platform

00:05:43.620 --> 00:05:46.430
with potential beyond other
mobile payment services.

00:05:46.430 --> 00:05:50.460
Today already, we allow
you to trade and buy stock

00:05:50.460 --> 00:05:53.800
on our platform and you could
invest in treasury bills

00:05:53.800 --> 00:05:56.310
and mutual funds, things like that, right?

00:05:56.310 --> 00:05:58.660
And all of that is from
a third-party provider,

00:05:58.660 --> 00:06:02.320
who is leveraging the open
APIs that we have built.

00:06:02.320 --> 00:06:03.450
And so for us, it's great,

00:06:03.450 --> 00:06:05.480
because we don't wanna
become an asset manager.

00:06:05.480 --> 00:06:07.640
But we are happy to
partner with asset managers

00:06:07.640 --> 00:06:10.273
to offer their services on our platform.

00:06:11.230 --> 00:06:12.800
While Paga will continue investing

00:06:12.800 --> 00:06:16.400
in local markets, they're
planning to expand beyond Nigeria.

00:06:16.400 --> 00:06:18.070
Mexico is our next market,

00:06:18.070 --> 00:06:21.610
and it's actually amazingly
similar to Nigeria.

00:06:21.610 --> 00:06:23.810
I feel like the Mexican
people are just Nigerians.

00:06:23.810 --> 00:06:28.230
It's like we're just very,
very similar, family-oriented,

00:06:28.230 --> 00:06:30.450
the savings collector,
like I described before,

00:06:30.450 --> 00:06:31.850
and they use cash.

00:06:31.850 --> 00:06:35.200
Same way, this is the 15th
largest economy in the world,

00:06:35.200 --> 00:06:37.240
but 90% of Mexicans don't even save

00:06:37.240 --> 00:06:38.970
at a formal financial institution.

00:06:38.970 --> 00:06:41.640
We're also launching in Ethiopia,

00:06:41.640 --> 00:06:44.210
where we actually already have
a team of about 60 people.

00:06:44.210 --> 00:06:46.540
That's a longer runway game;

00:06:46.540 --> 00:06:47.580
we're playing the long game there.

00:06:47.580 --> 00:06:50.550
Ethiopia is the second
largest, by population, country

00:06:50.550 --> 00:06:51.383
in Africa.

00:06:52.460 --> 00:06:55.610
So we are making real good headway

00:06:55.610 --> 00:06:59.900
in terms of solving the
problem for payments

00:06:59.900 --> 00:07:03.643
and access to financial
services to the mass market.

